.\" Manpage for exed

.TH EXED 1 "July 26, 2026" "exed 0.1.0" "Development Utility Manuals"
.SH NAME
\fBexed\fR \- find or create an executable and open it in an editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBexed\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fP] \fIEXECUTABLE_NAME\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBexed\fR finds an executable by name and opens it in an editor.
By default, names without a slash are searched in \fBPATH\fR.
Names containing a slash are treated as paths and must already be executable.
.PP
With \fB-c\fR, \fBexed\fR creates a local executable file instead of searching
\fBPATH\fR.
Created files get an interpreter line and executable permissions before they
are opened.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "\fB-a\fR"
Find all matching executables in \fBPATH\fR and prompt for which one to edit.
This option cannot be used with \fB-c\fR.
.IP "\fB-c\fR"
Create \fIEXECUTABLE_NAME\fR instead of finding it in \fBPATH\fR.
.IP "\fB-e \fIEDITOR\fR"
Choose the editor to open the file in.
If this option is not used, \fBexed\fR checks \fB$VISUAL\fR, and \fB$EDITOR\fR,
in that order.
If none are set, it defaults to \fBvi\fR.
The editor may include simple flags, such as \fB-e "code -w"\fR or
\fB-e "vim -p"\fR.
.IP "\fB-v\fR"
Print the selected editor and file location before opening.
.IP "\fB-V\fR"
Print version information and exit.
.IP "\fB-h\fR"
Display short help.
.SH CREATION OPTIONS
The following options can only be used with \fB-c\fR.
.IP "\fB-l \fILANGUAGE\fR"
Choose the language for the created file.
The default language is \fBbash\fR unless configured otherwise.
Unless \fB-n\fR is used, \fBexed\fR resolves \fILANGUAGE\fR from the current
\fBPATH\fR and writes that path in the interpreter line.
.IP "\fB-i \fIINTERPRETER\fR"
Use a custom interpreter path.
Pass only the path; \fBexed\fR adds \fB#!\fR automatically.
For example, if \fBbash\fR resolves to a Homebrew install but the script should
use \fB/bin/bash\fR, pass \fB-i /bin/bash\fR.
.IP "\fB-n\fR"
Use \fBenv\fR from \fBPATH\fR with \fILANGUAGE\fR instead of resolving the
interpreter path immediately.
.IP "\fB-N\fR"
Unset a configured \fBenv=true\fR option from \fB~/.exedrc\fR.
.IP "\fB-m \fIMODE\fR"
Set permissions for the created file.
By default, permissions are set to \fB755\fR.
Permissions must be a three-digit chmod-style value, such as \fB755\fR or
\fB700\fR, and the owner must have execute permission.
.IP "\fB-t \fITEMPLATE\fR"
Use a language-scoped template for the created file.
For example, \fB-l bash -t strict\fR searches for \fBbash/strict\fR.
If \fBtemplate_dir\fR is configured, that directory is searched before the
built-in templates.
.IP "\fB-T\fR"
Unset a configured \fBtemplate=\fITEMPLATE\fR option from \fB~/.exedrc\fR.
.IP "\fB-o \fIOPTION\fR"
Add an option after the interpreter line.
For example, \fB-o -x\fR can be used with \fBbash\fR.
.IP "\fB-I\fR"
Write \fBIFS=$'\\n'\fR below the interpreter line.
This option is accepted only with \fBbash\fR and \fBzsh\fR.
.IP "\fB-C\fR"
Clobber.
If the file exists, overwrite it without asking.
.IP "\fB-B\fR"
No-clobber.
If the file exists, report an error and exit.
.IP "\fB-O\fR"
Prompt before overwriting an existing file.
This is the default and is useful for overriding \fBclobber=true\fR in
\fB~/.exedrc\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBVISUAL\fR"
Second editor fallback checked when \fB-e\fR is not passed.
.IP "\fBEDITOR\fR"
Third editor fallback checked when \fB-e\fR is not passed.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
\fBexed\fR accepts a configuration file at \fB~/.exedrc\fR.
Available variables are:
.PP
.nf
all_in_path=true
editor=EDITOR
verbose=true
language=LANGUAGE
template=NAME
template_dir=DIR
perms=MODE
env=true
clobber=true
clobber_mode=clobber
clobber_mode=noclobber
clobber_mode=interactive
set_ifs=true
.fi
.PP
Boolean values are false by default.
The \fBeditor\fR value may include simple flags, such as
\fBeditor="code -w"\fR or \fBeditor="vim -p"\fR.
Command-line flags override configuration values where an override exists.
For example, \fB-N\fR unsets configured \fBenv=true\fR, and \fB-O\fR
restores the interactive overwrite prompt.
.SH TEMPLATES
Templates are searched by language and name.
With \fB-l bash -t strict\fR, \fBexed\fR looks for \fBbash/strict\fR; it does
not search templates for other languages.
If \fBtemplate_dir=DIR\fR is configured, \fBexed\fR checks
\fBDIR/LANGUAGE/TEMPLATE\fR before the built-in
\fBshare/exed/templates/LANGUAGE/TEMPLATE\fR, so personal templates can
override the defaults.
.PP
Templates are plain text files.
They may include \fB{{shebang}}\fR, \fB{{language}}\fR, and \fB{{file}}\fR.
When \fB{{shebang}}\fR is omitted, \fBexed\fR prepends the interpreter line
automatically.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
\fBexed\fR can be used as a standalone executable or through a sourceable shell
plugin.
The zsh plugin is \fBexed.plugin.zsh\fR.
The bash plugin is \fBexed.plugin.bash\fR.
These files add the repository's \fBbin\fR directory to \fBPATH\fR and register
shell completion.
They do not source the executable itself.
.PP
Completion files are also provided for manual installation:
.PP
.nf
share/zsh/site-functions/_exed
share/bash-completion/completions/exed
.fi
.SH INSTALLATION
The included \fBinstall.sh\fR script installs \fBexed\fR as a standalone
executable.
By default it symlinks the executable to \fB/usr/local/bin/exed\fR, templates to
\fB/usr/local/share/exed/templates\fR, the man page to
\fB/usr/local/share/man/man1/exed.1\fR, and completion files below
\fB/usr/local/share\fR.
Use \fBinstall.sh --user\fR for an installation under \fB$HOME/.local\fR, or
\fBinstall.sh --prefix \fIDIR\fR to choose another prefix.
